Let σ ^ \hat{\sigma} denote the predicted per-scene scale.
We report:
(i) the relative scale error | σ ^ − σ | / σ |\hat{\sigma}-\sigma|/\sigma (mean and median over scenes), the log-scale error | log 10 ⁡ σ ^ − log 10 ⁡ σ | |\log_{10}\hat{\sigma}-\log_{10}\sigma| over scenes with σ ^ > 0 \hat{\sigma}>0 , and the number of physically meaningless predictions σ ^ ≤ 0 \hat{\sigma}\leq 0 ;
(ii) the AoLP angular error , computed with wrap-around at period 180 ∘ 180^{\circ} and restricted to pixels with ground-truth DoLP > 0.1 >0.1 , since AoLP is undefined for unpolarized light;
(iii) Stokes self-consistency : the angular error between the directly predicted AoLP and the AoLP recomputed from the predicted s 1 , s 2 \mathrm{s}_{1},\mathrm{s}_{2} , and analogously the mean absolute error for DoLP and DoCP;
(iv) the physical-bound violation rate : the fraction of pixels whose predicted Stokes vectors violate Eq. ( 1 ), i.e., s 1 2 + s 2 2 + s 3 2 > s 0 \sqrt{\mathrm{s}_{1}^{2}+\mathrm{s}_{2}^{2}+\mathrm{s}_{3}^{2}}>\mathrm{s}_{0} .
We measure (iv) on Stokes-derived values because the directly predicted descriptor maps are bounded by their encoding.
